 # tally — Frequently Asked Questions
 
-## Is tally really free?
+## Getting Started
+
+### Do I need to install anything?
+
+No. tally runs on Python 3.9 or newer, which is already on most machines. Clone the
+repository and run:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli --help
+```
+
+If you want to type `tally` instead of the full command, add a shell alias:
+
+```
+alias tally="python -m tally.cli"
+```
+
+### What commands are available?
+
+| Command  | What it does                                   |
+|----------|------------------------------------------------|
+| `add`    | Record a new expense                           |
+| `report` | Category totals for a month                    |
+| `list`   | Individual expense entries for a month         |
+| `budget` | Spending vs your configured budgets            |
+| `stats`  | Overall lifetime summary (count, total, span)  |
+| `export` | Write everything to a CSV file                 |
+
+Pass `--help` to any subcommand for the full list of flags:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli add --help
+```
+
+---
+
+## Recording Expenses
+
+### How do I record an expense?
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli add 12.50 food --note "lunch"
+```
+
+The first two arguments are the amount and the category. The note is optional.
+tally prints a confirmation line with the expense ID and the stored US-dollar amount.
+
+### What categories can I use?
+
+The built-in categories are: **food**, **transport**, **rent**, **entertainment**, **other**.
+
+If none of these fit, use `other` and put the detail in a note.
+
+### Can I back-date an expense?
+
+Yes. Pass `--date YYYY-MM-DD`:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli add 45.00 transport --date 2024-03-15
+```
+
+If you leave off `--date`, today's date is used automatically.
+
+### Can I add a note to an expense?
+
+Yes. Use the `--note` flag with any text you like:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli add 8.50 food --note "airport coffee"
+```
+
+### Can I edit or delete an expense?
+
+Not via a command — there is no `edit` or `delete` subcommand. Because your data is
+stored in a plain JSON file (`tally.json`), you can open it in any text editor and
+make changes directly. The format is straightforward: one object per expense with
+fields for `id`, `date`, `amount_usd`, `category`, and `note`.
+
+---
+
+## Viewing Your Data
+
+### How do I see where my money went?
+
+`report` gives category totals for a month:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli report --month 2024-03
+```
+
+Narrow it to one category:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli report --month 2024-03 --category food
+```
+
+### What's the difference between `report` and `list`?
+
+`report` shows aggregated totals per category. `list` shows individual line items.
+Use `report` to see the big picture; use `list` when you want to find a specific
+entry or review exactly what you spent.
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli list --month 2024-03
+python -m tally.cli list --month 2024-03 --category food
+```
+
+Omit `--month` from either command and you get all expenses across every month.
+
+### How do I check whether I'm over budget?
+
+Use `budget`. It compares what you've spent against the monthly budgets configured
+in `tally/config.py` and marks each category as under, close, or over:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli budget --month 2024-03
+```
+
+The current monthly budgets are:
+
+| Category      | Budget (USD) |
+|---------------|-------------:|
+| food          |       600.00 |
+| transport     |       200.00 |
+| rent          |     1 500.00 |
+| entertainment |       150.00 |
+| other         |       300.00 |
+
+To change a budget, edit the `CATEGORY_BUDGETS` dictionary in `tally/config.py`.
+
+### How do I get a quick overview of everything?
+
+`stats` prints lifetime totals in one glance:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli stats
+```
+
+Output includes total expenses recorded, the sum in USD, the average per expense,
+and the date range covered.
+
+### How do I get my data into a spreadsheet?
+
+Use `export`:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli export --out my-expenses.csv
+```
+
+Omit `--out` to print the CSV to stdout. All amounts in the CSV are in US dollars.
+
+---
+
+## Currencies
+
+### Can I enter expenses in other currencies?
+
+Yes. Pass `--currency` with the ISO currency code:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli add 39 food --currency EUR --date 2024-03-04
+```
+
+tally converts the amount to US dollars when it saves the record. The original
+amount and currency are kept for reference, but every total you see is in USD.
+
+### Which currencies are supported?
+
+EUR, GBP, INR, JPY, SGD, and USD. If you try a code not in that list, tally
+exits with an error rather than silently recording a wrong amount.
+
+### Do the exchange rates update automatically?
+
+No. The rates are fixed constants baked into `tally/rates.py`. They are not
+fetched from the internet, so tally works offline. If a rate has drifted far
+from reality, you can update it in `rates.py` — but be aware this changes
+the US-dollar totals for every past expense that used that currency.
+
+---
+
+## Your Data File
+
+### Where is my data stored?
+
+In a file called `tally.json` in whatever directory you run the command from.
+Everything is plain JSON — you can open it in any text editor, diff it with git,
+or copy it anywhere.
+
+### Can I use a different file or location?
+
+Yes. Pass `--store` before the subcommand:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli --store ~/Documents/finances/tally.json add 12.50 food
+```
+
+This lets you maintain separate files for separate purposes (personal vs. business,
+for example). You can also drop a shell alias for each.
+
+### How do I back up my data?
+
+Copy `tally.json` anywhere — to a cloud folder, a USB drive, or a git repository
+alongside your dotfiles. Because it is plain text, it diffs cleanly and compresses
+well.
+
